WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.966 --> 00:00:02.034
Every now and then,
a monster comes around

00:00:02.034 --> 00:00:03.536
that's a real "head-scratcher."

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:06.705
 Siren Head's exaggerated
 humanoid shape

00:00:06.705 --> 00:00:08.307
 is scary, sure,

00:00:08.307 --> 00:00:11.243
 but it's the inexplicable
 horn-shaped speakers

00:00:11.243 --> 00:00:13.445
 emanating constant
 blaring noise

00:00:13.445 --> 00:00:16.115
 that invokes
 an unusual brand of terror

00:00:16.115 --> 00:00:17.950
 as it looms over landscapes.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.187
 While you could technically
 call this monster "humanoid,"

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:24.257
 there's no doubt it's also
 an Eldridge being--

00:00:24.257 --> 00:00:26.959
 a weird, eerie,
 malevolent force.

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:29.095
 Known for its impressive
 hunting skills,

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:30.763
 despite its lack of eyes,

00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:34.367
 the origins of its anatomy
 remain largely a mystery--

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.870
 perfect fodder
 for digital folklore.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.539
Siren Head exists
in illusory realism,

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:42.408
which makes its appearance
in digitally altered images

00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:44.777
in videos
that much more convincing.

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:47.480
And we are invited
to participate in the expansion

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.550
and spread of its lore
through digital interactions.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.186
 Since its digital creation
 in 2018,

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:55.722
 online gaming communities,
 YouTube creators,

00:00:55.722 --> 00:00:59.392
 artists and TikTokers
 have embraced Siren Head,

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:01.661
 building creepy worlds
 for it to inhabit.

00:01:01.661 --> 00:01:05.064
So what does Siren Head's
growing social media presence

00:01:05.064 --> 00:01:07.634
tell us about folklore
in the digital age?

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.670
 I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.072
 and this is "Monstrum."

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:17.277
On August 19, 2018,
at 11:09 am,

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:20.512
a user Tweeted a grainy photo
of a strange being

00:01:20.512 --> 00:01:23.482
accompanied by a short
eye-witness account.

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:25.751
 According
 to this first narrative,

00:01:25.751 --> 00:01:28.754
 while sightseeing graveyards,
 as one does,

00:01:28.754 --> 00:01:31.224
 a woman saw it motionless.

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:33.092
 Initially,
 she thought the thing--

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:35.294
 as big as an old
 macabre telephone pole--

00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:37.096
 was an art installation...

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:39.198
 that is,
 before it blared to life

00:01:39.198 --> 00:01:41.300
 with sound buzzing out,

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.369
 "Nine, eighteen, one,

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:46.238
 child, seventeen,
 remove, vile."

00:01:46.238 --> 00:01:48.674
 Then it started
 walking towards her.

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:51.110
 That's it--that's where
 her story ends

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.413
 and the legend
 of Siren Head begins.

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:56.416
So, how is a modern monster
even born?

00:01:56.416 --> 00:01:59.586
It's rare I get to talk
to an actual monster creator.

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:02.288
After all, most of the creatures
covered here on "Monstrum"

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:04.757
go so far back
into human history,

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:07.794
a lot of the original content
was made hundreds

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:09.395
or thousands of years ago.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:11.397
 So, outside of a séance,
 I have little chance

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:14.801
of getting any face time
with the earliest storytellers.

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.435
 But not this time.

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:20.072
I got the chance to talk to
Siren Head's original creator,

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:22.441
horror artist
Trevor Henderson.

00:02:22.441 --> 00:02:26.345
So, Siren Head is probably
my most popular character.

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:29.148
 He really blew up
 on YouTube

00:02:29.148 --> 00:02:31.150
 and all kinds
 of social media sites

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:33.419
 and really caught on
 with a lot of people.

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:35.388
 I was really obsessed
 with the concept

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:37.290
 of number stations
 at the time.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.193
 Number stations are
 these sort of anomalous,

00:02:41.193 --> 00:02:43.596
 very ominous,
 scary broadcasts

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:45.798
 that are still happening
 today on the radio waves,

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:50.736
 that are frequently just,
 like, chanting numbers

00:02:50.736 --> 00:02:52.138
 and random words

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:54.273
 and nursery-rhyme
 kind of jingles.

00:02:54.273 --> 00:02:57.243
 The leading theory on them
 is that they are

00:02:57.243 --> 00:03:00.379
 abandoned spy broadcasts
 from the world wars.

00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:02.815
But nobody really knows
for sure exactly what they are,

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:05.184
and I thought that was
a really mysterious concept,

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:07.252
and, you know, I thought
that would be really cool

00:03:07.252 --> 00:03:08.721
as a basis for a monster.

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:10.489
 (Dr. Zarka)
 Roughly 40 feet tall,

00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:12.725
 Siren Head gains
 its distinctive name

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:13.926
 from its odd head--

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:17.329
 two metal horn-style speakers.

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:19.265
 These speakers
 emit constant noise--

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:21.601
strange music,
snippets of human conversation,

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:24.304
 electrical sounds,
 random numbers, and words;

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:26.606
 even white noise
 while it sleeps.

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:31.043
 Its body is an elongated,
 long-limbed humanoid skeleton

00:03:31.043 --> 00:03:32.711
 covered in mummified skin,

00:03:32.711 --> 00:03:36.749
with the occasional electrical
wire appearing under the flesh.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:38.250
 While it has no eyes,

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.452
 it's an incredibly
 effective predator.

00:03:40.452 --> 00:03:44.223
And it kind of wanders through
the Pacific Northwest woods.

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:47.626
 It can mimic people
 and other sounds

00:03:47.626 --> 00:03:49.762
 and animals
 to lure in its victims,

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:51.230
 which it disposes of.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:52.865
 (Dr. Zarka)
 The extremely long arms

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:56.402
 and ability to stand
 perfectly motionless for days

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:58.770
 allow it to blend
 into even forests,

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.840
 despite its appearance
 as a streetlight

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.308
 or a telephone pole.

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.777
 It often stalks rural towns,
 dusty roads,

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:07.613
 and seemingly
 abandoned landscapes.

00:04:07.613 --> 00:04:09.515
Like so many monsters
before it,

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:11.917
Siren Head's notoriety
grew as its presence

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:13.819
in popular culture increased,

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:16.722
and video games offered
a ready-made stage.

00:04:16.722 --> 00:04:20.559
 In 2018, a short game
 produced by Modus Interactive,

00:04:20.559 --> 00:04:23.596
 called "Siren Head,"
 featured the creature.

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:27.099
 You play as a forest ranger
 who has to evade Siren Head

00:04:27.099 --> 00:04:29.335
 while searching
 for a lost hiker.

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:30.870
 Then, in 2019,

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.040
 gamers playing the "Fallout 4"
 mod Whispering Hills

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.276
could see and hear the creature
in a fog-covered world

00:04:37.276 --> 00:04:39.679
 broadcasting static,
 claxon alarms,

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:41.981
and emergency message warnings.

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:43.516
 Videos of startled gamers

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:45.351
 stumbling upon
 the in-game creature

00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:47.987
 can be found
 all over the Internet.

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:52.425
Siren Head reached a new level
in 2020 thanks to TikTok.

00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:55.461
 On April 30, digital artist
 Alex Howard

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.897
 posted a video of Siren Head
 over a soundtrack

00:04:57.897 --> 00:05:01.534
 of blaring police sirens
 and muted gunfire--

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:03.469
 the palm-tree-lined
 city location

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:07.105
 taking the rural monster
 into an urban space.

00:05:07.105 --> 00:05:09.575
The post got 23 million views

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:12.578
and 3.3 million likes
in a week.

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:14.814
And it really contributed
to Siren Head becoming,

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:18.484
like, this extremely strange
viral phenomenon out of nowhere,

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:20.319
that I could
never have predicted,

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:23.255
and I never would have
predicted.

00:05:23.255 --> 00:05:25.524
It's still very baffling to me
even now.

00:05:25.524 --> 00:05:26.993
If you've ever been on TikTok,

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:29.428
you'll already know that
repetition and imitation

00:05:29.428 --> 00:05:30.830
are foundational.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.999
What does that mean
for horror?

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:36.602
There's an active interest in
not just sharing spooky videos

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:39.071
but contributing to them
in some way,

00:05:39.071 --> 00:05:42.475
making it easy to shape an
emerging monster's development.

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:44.910
Think of it
as crowd-sourced horror.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:47.446
In a way, many
of the original monster videos

00:05:47.446 --> 00:05:50.282
found on TikTok
could also be considered

00:05:50.282 --> 00:05:52.484
an emerging subgenre
of folk horror.

00:05:52.484 --> 00:05:55.220
 While Siren Head
 is decidedly modern,

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:56.822
 its old-fashioned speakers

00:05:56.822 --> 00:05:59.758
 and original rural,
 remote hunting-ground spaces

00:05:59.758 --> 00:06:02.895
 help us conceptualize
 the monster as legendary

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:05.331
 or at least,
 a relic of the past.

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:07.033
 And in a very meta way,

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:09.869
 TikTok might also be
 the isolating space

00:06:09.869 --> 00:06:12.504
 where the storytelling
 that contributes to Siren Head

00:06:12.504 --> 00:06:15.207
 occurs in a futuristic
 folkloresque.

00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:18.777
 The very idea that folklore
 can even be transmitted

00:06:18.777 --> 00:06:21.680
and performed in digital spaces
is a new one.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:24.350
 Sure, the volume of people
 editing photographs,

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.319
 re-treating scary stories,

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:28.353
 or posting creative
 TikTok horror shorts

00:06:28.353 --> 00:06:29.922
 may be massive in scale,

00:06:29.922 --> 00:06:33.692
 but behind almost every post
 is a real person

00:06:33.692 --> 00:06:35.461
 whose contribution
 to the genre

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:37.096
 shapes these modern monsters.

00:06:37.096 --> 00:06:40.565
 As new manifestations
 are crafted and distributed,

00:06:40.565 --> 00:06:42.468
 people add their own twists,

00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:44.636
 based on their own fears
 and anxieties,

00:06:44.636 --> 00:06:47.140
 contributing
 to digital folk culture.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:50.075
How it's kind of been
adopted by

00:06:50.075 --> 00:06:53.846
a lot of YouTubers
and people online for children.

00:06:53.846 --> 00:06:57.716
 I mean, I never designed
 Siren Head for kids.

00:06:57.716 --> 00:07:00.452
 It's really cool that it's
 resonated with so many kids,

00:07:00.452 --> 00:07:04.556
but, you know,
I hope I'm not giving anyone

00:07:04.556 --> 00:07:06.425
sleepless nights or whatnot.

00:07:06.425 --> 00:07:07.726
 (Dr. Zarka)
 The question, of course, is:

00:07:07.726 --> 00:07:10.029
 What is Siren Head
 a symbol of?

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:12.899
 What fears or anxieties
 about this moment in time

00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:14.200
 does it represent?

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.003
I just wanted to heighten
how strange

00:07:17.003 --> 00:07:18.737
I could make the creature,

00:07:18.737 --> 00:07:20.839
and make it feel very
not-of-this-world--

00:07:20.839 --> 00:07:25.210
 like having human features,
 such just teeth or arms

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:29.448
 on something that's clearly
 maybe not 100% organic,

00:07:29.448 --> 00:07:33.385
 and incorporating technology
 or broken malfunctioning bits

00:07:33.385 --> 00:07:35.487
 of machinery into it.

00:07:35.487 --> 00:07:37.623
 It all added--it was all
 to add up and make it

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:41.761
 as disconcerting and scary,
 I guess, as possible.

00:07:41.761 --> 00:07:43.395
 (Dr. Zarka)
 I find the telephone pole

00:07:43.395 --> 00:07:45.864
 or streetlight comparisons
 interesting.

00:07:45.864 --> 00:07:47.999
 Unlike other monsters
 throughout history,

00:07:47.999 --> 00:07:50.636
 or even more modern ones
 like Slender Man,

00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:54.807
Siren Head is inherently linked
to our use of technology,

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:57.343
 as indicated by
 its preferred disguises

00:07:57.343 --> 00:07:59.579
 as a telephone pole
 or streetlight,

00:07:59.579 --> 00:08:01.213
 not to mention
 its speaker head.

00:08:01.213 --> 00:08:02.748
 (Henderson)
 A lot of people draw parallels

00:08:02.748 --> 00:08:04.750
 between Siren Head
 and Slender Man.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:06.652
 I think Slender Man's
 a great monster.

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:08.020
 So any comparisons,
 you know,

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.655
 I think that's
 a great compliment.

00:08:09.655 --> 00:08:10.990
 (Dr. Zarka)
 Fear of technology

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:12.057
 is a common trope,

00:08:12.057 --> 00:08:14.593
 but the old-timey look
 of Siren Head

00:08:14.593 --> 00:08:16.695
 suggests an uneasy nostalgia.

00:08:16.695 --> 00:08:19.464
 We look back into the past
 with new eyes,

00:08:19.464 --> 00:08:20.800
 or our phone cameras,

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.169
 and find new horrors
 along the way.

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:25.170
 I also can't help
 thinking of Siren Head

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:27.005
 as a representative
 boundary creature

00:08:27.005 --> 00:08:29.675
 akin to the original concept
 of the troll,

00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:31.911
 which is a label
 for any antisocial creature

00:08:31.911 --> 00:08:35.580
 that occupies a space outside
 of so-called civilization

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:37.449
 and poses a threat
 to the living things

00:08:37.449 --> 00:08:38.717
 within the community.

00:08:38.717 --> 00:08:40.386
 Siren Head occupies spaces

00:08:40.386 --> 00:08:43.689
 that are becoming increasingly
 less inhabited by humans--

00:08:43.689 --> 00:08:46.525
 environments heavily impacted
 by economic factors

00:08:46.525 --> 00:08:47.993
 and urban encroachment.

00:08:47.993 --> 00:08:50.662
 Siren Head is the result
 of imagination

00:08:50.662 --> 00:08:53.598
 and artistic interpretations
 of fear.

00:08:53.598 --> 00:08:55.934
 Folklore's evolution
 in popular culture

00:08:55.934 --> 00:08:58.671
 makes new monsters
 like this one stand out

00:08:58.671 --> 00:09:01.339
 alongside more traditional,
 rigid structures

00:09:01.339 --> 00:09:03.141
 or sequential stories.

00:09:03.141 --> 00:09:05.544
Digital folklore is
more like an amalgamation

00:09:05.544 --> 00:09:08.614
of superstitions
and preternatural possibilities

00:09:08.614 --> 00:09:11.550
borrowed from a variety
of traditions.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:13.886
Folklore changes
through time and space,

00:09:13.886 --> 00:09:16.822
picking up variations
in the way it is told

00:09:16.822 --> 00:09:18.491
and retold and by whom,

00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:22.395
which can be a really fruitful
mode for creative exploration.

00:09:22.395 --> 00:09:24.931
And I think social media
is definitely the modern-day--

00:09:24.931 --> 00:09:27.433
a huge facet, sorry,
in the modern-day folklore,

00:09:27.433 --> 00:09:29.001
 and Creepypasta is, like,

00:09:29.001 --> 00:09:30.969
 kind of the biggest example
 of that, I guess.

00:09:30.969 --> 00:09:33.672
 I think we miss
 that communal storytelling

00:09:33.672 --> 00:09:36.308
 that we used to have
 a lot more

00:09:36.308 --> 00:09:38.911
 before social media--

00:09:38.911 --> 00:09:40.946
 you know, like telling stories
 around the campfire, maybe.

00:09:40.946 --> 00:09:44.550
And, you know, it's the closest
approximation of that

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:47.152
 is being able to throw stories
 and monsters

00:09:47.152 --> 00:09:50.188
 back and forth online,
 on Twitter or Instagram.

00:09:50.188 --> 00:09:53.425
Like the creature's reported
ability to blend easily

00:09:53.425 --> 00:09:54.860
into its environment,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:57.563
the adaptability of modern
Internet monster lore

00:09:57.563 --> 00:09:59.030
is significant.

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:01.900
Still, there is a self-awareness
to Siren Head.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:04.770
No one is sincerely arguing
that this monster exists

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:06.138
in the real world.

00:10:06.138 --> 00:10:09.174
Instead, it serves
as self-conscious fiction

00:10:09.174 --> 00:10:13.279
that explores the boundaries
of art, technology, and fear.

00:10:13.279 --> 00:10:14.613
I'm totally honored.

00:10:14.613 --> 00:10:16.782
 And the idea that kids
 could be inspired

00:10:16.782 --> 00:10:18.951
 by my monsters
 and my art

00:10:18.951 --> 00:10:21.053
 to do their own monsters,
 which will inevitably be

00:10:21.053 --> 00:10:23.656
 way cooler and better
 eventually,

00:10:23.656 --> 00:10:26.959
 it's totally--
 it's a total honor.

00:10:26.959 --> 00:10:30.195
And I'm just so happy that

00:10:30.195 --> 00:10:31.897
Siren Head could be that

00:10:31.897 --> 00:10:34.400
for a whole lot
of different kids.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:35.835
 It's super cool.

00:10:38.436 --> 00:10:39.671
 (speaker)
 Ready?

00:10:39.671 --> 00:10:42.174
I'm ready. That slimy
swamp ghost himself...

00:10:42.174 --> 00:10:43.476
Let's do it again.

00:10:43.476 --> 00:10:44.743
Oh, okay, yeah.

00:10:44.743 --> 00:10:46.278
But it's an illusion.

00:10:46.278 --> 00:10:48.681
[laughs]

